1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:37,680
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:46,080
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard, and with me today, RevenueCat CEO, Jacob Biding.

7
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:58,160
Our guest today is Anton Derlaitka, co-founder and CEO of Sweatcoin, one of the largest health and fitness apps in the world, the past three years, with more than 180 million registered users.

8
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:12,320
On the podcast, we talk with Anton about building sustainable viral growth loops at scale, how to effectively gamify user incentives, and why emotional connections drive more value than purely economic ones.

9
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:15,760
Hey Anton, thank you so much for joiningining us on the podcast today.

10
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:17,680
Yeah, thank you so much for having me.

11
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:18,240
Pleasure.

12
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:20,560
And Jacob, nice to have you on as well.

13
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:22,080
Excited to be here.

14
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,880
So Anton, I wanted to kick things off with a little bit about Sweatcoin.

15
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:35,280
I don't think our audience will be as familiar with Sweatcoin as they are with some other apps, especially because you're just now starting to layer subscriptions on.

16
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:38,880
So it's not kind of well known in the subscription app industry.

17
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:42,160
But I think you have a lot of interesting things we're going to be able to talk about today.

18
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:43,680
But why don't you just kick it off?

19
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:47,280
Tell us a little bit about Sweatcoin, how it was founded and what the app is.

20
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:48,320
Yeah, thank you.

21
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:51,600
So Sweatcoin has a very simple concept as a core.

22
00:01:52,080 --> 00:02:01,680
We are great believers that physical activity is valuable and we wanted to create something that would help people develop a very healthy habit of continuously moving.

23
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:04,640
We all know that it's very, very hard to be active.

24
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:10,000
We've all been on the receiving end of that early January Blue Monday type of depression.

25
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:19,840
You buy a gym membership, you give up, you install a new app and you promise yourself that you're going to work out like three times a week and then you, before you know it, you've forgotten about it.

26
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:21,600
So I mean, we all know this, right?

27
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:24,240
You know, building a habit is difficult.

28
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:25,600
Why is it so?

29
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:32,480
And you know, the funny thing, and that's one of the things that we can kind of discovered very early is actually we're not born to be active.

30
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:34,000
We're born to be lazy.

31
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:38,960
Mother Nature didn't ever think that we will have excess calories at some point in time.

32
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:50,000
So actually, it didn't build in any kind of behavioral mechanisms that would encourage you to be active, which is very different to some other behaviors, like take procreation.

33
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:51,360
We have organs.

34
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:52,480
So we procreate, right?

35
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:53,600
So I mean, easy.

36
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:56,720
But well, as it turns out, we don't have organs for exercise.

37
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:03,360
And we almost kind of, obviously, the whole thing, I'm just making a bit of a joke, but we I was really kind of fascinated.

38
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:07,280
You know, I've been in the sports fitness space for many years.

39
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:14,880
And one of the things that was constantly fascinated by is that, you know, why motivations are not there for some people and they are for some others.

40
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:19,440
So obviously that the biggest and the best thing about this is intrinsic motivation.

41
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:21,920
And many of us actually don't have a problem with that.

42
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:22,880
And we're really lucky.

43
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:27,280
Truth is that about seventy percent of the world population don't have that ability.

44
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:38,160
And they need to be approached because, you know, all of the fitness industry is based on that pretty much that very healthy concept of, yeah, you get, you need to get more responsible, you need to get moving and everything.

45
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:41,280
And that's why, you know, there's so much success.

46
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:43,840
You look around yourself, everybody's doing something.

47
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,320
Everybody's going for a walk to the gym stuff.

48
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:50,080
But the reality is 70% of the world population do not bind to this.

49
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:52,400
So we wanted to come up with something different.

50
00:03:52,960 --> 00:04:01,680
We wanted to come up with something less intrusive, not like guild-based or whatever guild trip-based type of things, you know, lazy bums, stand up, do a 5k and all this kind of jazz.

51
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:06,080
And, you know, obviously loyalty programs had been around for ages.

52
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:07,360
They're popular.

53
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:12,720
And the initial idea was, can we actually come up with some sort of loyalty program, air miles for steps?

54
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:14,400
And this is where it all started.

55
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:16,800
But that's kind of loyalty programs are not cool.

56
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:18,800
You know, you look at the air miles.

57
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:31,280
And then, you know, so obviously we started, I started iterating, trying to come up with a different level of user engagement that could actually make people adopt a new habit.

58
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:35,680
And then, as I said, you know, my initial claim is that physical activity is valuable.

59
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:48,400
And the idea was just very simply that, you know, physical activity is valuable literally because people who are more physically active, they are more productive, they are healthier, they destroy less value for the economy generally because they get ill less frequently.

60
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:59,120
And if we were able to capture that value one way or another and somehow tokenize it and share it back with the user, it would create more val a ongoing flywheel.

61
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:00,560
And that's exactly what we did.

62
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:02,480
So yes, that's a long story.

63
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:06,560
You know, that story kind of materialized initially in the form of a loyalty program.

64
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,320
Then I started chatting with a few friends.

65
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:15,360
I think that I ended up speaking to my now co-founder who was like, Oh man, let's just take it all the way.

66
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:16,640
Let's call it a coin.

67
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:19,120
And well, of course, we were inspired by Bitcoin then.

68
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:20,480
It was back in 2015.

69
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:23,440
Bitcoin was still in its infancy, relative infancy.

70
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:30,400
So we came up with this concept of creating like a virtual kind of point system or token system.

71
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,720
The next thing we knew is that it just exploded.

72
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:35,200
That whole idea is super fascinating.

73
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:38,240
You know, a lot of people these days are trying to recreate Duolingo.

74
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,640
They do Duolingo for this or Duolingo for that.

75
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:45,280
And, you know, Streaks is kind of an interesting way to do that.

76
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:55,600
But this whole idea of like this loyalty program of a coin is an interesting kind of spin in the other direction of incentivizing those things.

77
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:57,520
So how does Sweatcoin actually work?

78
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:01,440
And, lurking enough, I was telling my wife last night that I was interviewing the CEO of Sweatcoin.

79
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:03,760
She's like, Sweatcoin, that sounds kind of gross.

80
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:06,640
And I was like, Yes, but it's also really cool.

81
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:08,160
It's like you earn coins.

82
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:09,680
I didn't think that for a second.

83
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:12,720
For sweating, which is, uh, I think a really cool idea.

84
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:14,400
So, how does that actually work?

85
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:20,160
And maybe it would be interesting even to talk through some early iterations on like what did work and what didn't work.

86
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:22,960
The question I was going to ask is like, how did you bootstrap that fly?

87
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:27,440
You know, you talk about like exercise and sweat being inherently valuable, right?

88
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:32,720
But actually creating there needs to be some arbitrage there that actually like capturing and sort of tokenizing that.

89
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:37,760
So, like, how did you go about bootstraping the value for the customer and then also for the partners?

90
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:39,840
Yeah, brilliant question, like a set of questions.

91
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,360
So let me ask them one by one.

92
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:45,600
So, I think, first of all, on the name, our original working title was Fitcoin.

93
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:50,640
And then we thought it wasn't like like it was too cosy, almost like meaningless.

94
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:52,720
We needed something a bit more polarizing.

95
00:06:53,440 --> 00:06:59,840
And a lot of people, especially in the early days right now, it's become a little bit of a, you know, a lot of people have heard about it and they don't have a problem.

96
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:02,800
Like here in the UK, we have this brand called Sweaty Betty.

97
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:09,680
And, you know, it's become such a kind of high street phenomenon that nobody thinks about Sweaty Betty.

98
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:11,200
Everybody talks Sweaty Betty.

99
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:11,600
That's it.

100
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:12,080
That's fine.

101
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:14,000
It's similar with Sweatcoin.

102
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:15,360
Now, and I love the name.

103
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:20,240
Just to be clear, like what was cool last night in the conversation with my wife was that it was polarizing.

104
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:22,240
And it, like, it led to a conversation.

105
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:23,600
Like, I said Sweatcoin.

106
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:25,920
She said, ooh, that's gross or weird.

107
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:31,280
And then we had a whole conversation about like sweating and like earning for sweat and stuff like that.

108
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:35,120
So I think it's ultimately like a really successful name in that way.

109
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:36,240
We'll have it too.

110
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:36,480
Yeah.

111
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:41,440
So, but, you know, the original kind of the starting, the app is very simple.

112
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:43,920
You walk, we make sure that those steps are real.

113
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:55,440
So we run a piece of software that we call a validator, actually ensures that, you know, you can't put it on a dog, as some people suggest, on top of a washing machine or create a farm in Vietnam and, you know, like fake the system.

114
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,560
And then, you know, every thousand steps give you a sweat coin.

115
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:06,960
And then you can turn those sweat coins into product, services, experiences, donate to charity and send to your friends.

116
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:10,240
So that's a very simplistic type of activity.

117
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:13,600
Now coming back to your original question about gamification.

118
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:16,960
Now, look, gamification has been around for ages.

119
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:28,640
I think the first time I kind of consciously heard about it, I think was back in, I want to say maybe 2005, when I had a course on Coursera called Gamification.

120
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:33,040
And all that kind of jazz, the streaks, the badges, leaderboards, it was all there.

121
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:37,120
So the human kind has known about gamification for ages.

122
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:39,040
But it's just easier said than done.

123
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:41,040
So I think Duolingo did really, really well.

124
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:43,440
What they do is a different toolkit.

125
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,160
They do gamification really, really well.

126
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:51,760
And we went slightly down, down a slightly different route, which is incentives, the use of incentives.

127
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:59,200
Because one thing that fitness kind of lacks a little bit is that all the fitness apps, they're kind of similar.

128
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:03,040
You are being asked to kind of do something that's generally unnatural.

129
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:07,840
You don't, you don't want, especially for those people who are not fitness junkies, but, you know, yeah, just do something.

130
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:09,200
And that's it.

131
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:13,600
We wanted to take a slightly different angle and kind of make it fun.

132
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:19,520
Essentially, the promise there is that look, you know, you walk anyway, why don't you try and earn something useful?

133
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:22,000
So it's a very different concept, very different message.

134
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:26,960
When we just launched, we intentionally, the product concept was that we didn't want to look like a fitness app.

135
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:38,480
So the look and feel was much more of a financial app where you had the kind of the wallet and you had a marketplace and the techy, very techy look.

136
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:41,360
We had a very cool kind of generative graphics there.

137
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:52,720
It was cold and it was a bit we definitely pay some tributes to the kind of the emerging crypto scene there because we wanted to kind of congratulate the new movement and stuff like that.

138
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:58,720
So all of that essentially we became the only financially looking app in health and fitness.

139
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:01,760
So I think that created a lot of buzz.

140
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:03,600
That was really, really interesting.

141
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:06,560
Again, don't forget that we'll launch the app in 2016.

142
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:10,320
It was very early days of crypto and some people would just, you know, have no idea.

143
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:15,920
And then I think our first stage of growth, obviously timing lucky as many products are.

144
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:18,560
We launched in the ICO boom of 2017.

145
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:26,880
But I think what was really, really important there as well, we launched and then the press, the media picked up on the story because it was fun.

146
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:28,400
A lot of people wrote about us.

147
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:33,200
We got our first initial whatever, thirty, forty thousand users, which was great, amazing.

148
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:37,360
But then obviously we hit a bit of a wall in terms of how do you continue to grow?

149
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:43,920
And, you know, funny, I remember we applied to Y Combinator, we made it to the very final round, and then we got rejected.

150
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:56,480
And they said, guys, you need to grow faster because we were so happy and we were so proud that our retention was skyrocketed, you know, rocketing in terms of other health and fitness products because health and fitness is really difficult to retain users.

151
00:10:56,720 --> 00:10:59,920
And we were doing so well on our first kind of fifty thousand user base.

152
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:01,360
So we thought that was good enough.

153
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:03,040
But they said, no, that's not good enough.

154
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:04,320
You guys need to figure out how to grow.

155
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:09,840
So we came back to the drawing board.ard that was November, I want to say November 16.

156
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:14,720
And we said, look, you know, we need to get to like 300 users every day.

157
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:18,160
And then we came up with this idea to actually turn things around.

158
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:27,920
We just realized that unlike other apps or products that actually, if you want to build a referral program, you all of a sudden you need to have quite a lot of cash to be able to fund it.

159
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:30,800
Now we didn't have to do it because we had our own currency.

160
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:33,360
We were our own central bank, so to speak.

161
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:36,240
So we created a user journey, a very simplistic one.

162
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:42,240
So if you bought into this idea, you install the app, you see a very simplistic today's screen.

163
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,880
There are two things you can do to earn sweatcoins.

164
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:48,480
You can either start walking or you invite a friend for five sweatcoins.

165
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:56,640
And that created quite a lot of that intrinsic variality where we made the earning sweatcoins essentially earning sweatcoins is the user experience.

166
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:04,320
Of course, as in any kind of gaming economics, you need to provide ways for people to do something with those coins once they've been earned.

167
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:06,320
But that happens usually later.

168
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:08,400
And this is how we solved the cold start problem.

169
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:14,560
So we've created that inherent loop that's ingrained into the core user experience.

170
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:17,200
I think this is what's so special about our growth engine.

171
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:19,600
And that kind of helped us to get over the line.

172
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:25,120
and we got to 300 users a day and then 1000 and then 5000.

173
00:12:25,280 --> 00:12:34,960
And then, as I said, gradually that, you know, the train gained speed and we ended up just racing around and about nine months later from Goodwood Capital.

174
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:42,960
Curiously enough, one of my interns about nine months later in the summer of 2017, he said, Do you guys mind if I reapply to Y Combinator?

175
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:47,360
I said, I don't think it's needed, but hey, why don't you go ahead and do it.

176
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:50,240
So he did and we ended up being accepted.

177
00:12:50,480 --> 00:12:52,880
By that time, we already kind of signed a term sheet.

178
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:59,600
I really wanted to get into Y Combinator, but on our terms, that wasn't acceptable for them, so we kind of passed on them.

179
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:00,560
So it's a funny one.

180
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:02,640
Things changed in twelve months a lot.

181
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:05,360
So no, but I think that, you know, credit to them.

182
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:07,200
I think the conversation was really useful.

183
00:13:07,520 --> 00:13:08,640
Just a point of clarification.

184
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:13,520
So from the beginning, this like sweatcoin, is it a literal crypto coin?

185
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:14,640
Like, is it on a chain?

186
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:16,640
No, it's like a virtual currency essentially.

187
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:19,920
At the moment we launched, there were only two blockchains out there.

188
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:21,280
The first is Bitcoin.

189
00:13:21,520 --> 00:13:21,760
Right.

190
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:23,680
Ethereum was like just starting.

191
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:25,680
Ethereum was just a startup.

192
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:30,880
We met with Talik and went for a walk down the river Thames, the South Bank.

193
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:33,520
He walks very, very fast, by the way.

194
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:39,200
But we concluded that, you know, we were not in a position to build our own blockchain.

195
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:39,600
No need.

196
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:39,920
Yeah.

197
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:41,360
I mean, I think it would have been then.

198
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:43,840
Obviously, the user experience challenges were crazy.

199
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:44,400
They still are.

200
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:45,520
It was slow.

201
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:46,480
It was expensive.

202
00:13:46,720 --> 00:13:47,440
It was like everything.

203
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:59,360
So it was only in 2022 when the technology was of age and we were ready to essentially partner with Sweat Foundation to launch a crypto proposition, which is a separate thing to Sweat Coins.

204
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:00,640
There's no connection.

205
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:07,680
But we are partnering the organization and some of our users who actually decide to opt in, they can convert their steps into a Sweat token.

206
00:14:07,920 --> 00:14:08,800
But that's a separate story.

207
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:10,800
Sweat Coin continues to be a health and fitness app.

208
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:13,200
It's disconnected from Sweat Foundation.

209
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:15,600
But it's essentially a fiat currency, right?

210
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:17,040
With the app, a sweat coin, right?

211
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:17,440
You control.

212
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,720
Well, technically speaking, it's an in-app currency.

213
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:29,360
And then our job is just to make sure that it's useful for our users to make that sweat coin, you know, to earn that sweat coin because they find it useful.

