00:14:56,640 --> 00:15:01,920
But for him now, there is direct, like, monetary value to him doing exercise.

221
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:04,560
And that's a, that's highly incentivized for him.

222
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:14,080
How did you early on and then how has it evolved in making the sweatcoin actually valuable and something that people actually want and are incentivized to get?

223
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:16,720
So look, you know, let me rephrase the question again.

224
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:20,080
So a lot of people basically question, okay, how is that possible?

225
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:21,440
Where does the value come from?

226
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:28,800
And when we just started this, obviously we researched the graveyard.ard of other companies that tried to do something similar and what they failed.

227
00:15:29,040 --> 00:15:33,200
You know, the most obvious answer there is that okay, I need to find a backer.

228
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:44,160
You know, I need to find someone who provides the value because in your example, you are the backer of the whatever that kind of the value that your son is creating by being active.

229
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:47,600
And the most obvious answers are one of the three.

230
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:53,760
It's either a health care provider, it's a health care insurer, it's a health insurer, or it's an employer.

231
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:57,520
All those stakeholders are potentially interested in you being active.

232
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:03,200
The approach with that, you know, it wasn't and we were not excited about any of the three.

233
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:11,840
As you know, I mean, when you start up trying to penetrate the healthcare system unless you've got some incredible, some competitive advantage, nearly impossible.

234
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:13,840
Same applies to health insurance.

235
00:16:14,160 --> 00:16:17,760
And with employer, you know, corporate wellness is a red ocean.

236
00:16:17,920 --> 00:16:20,080
It's a very busy area, lots of competition.

237
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:23,600
You know, the check size is small, revenue retention is low.

238
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:25,040
It's hard to compete.

239
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:27,520
So we kind of went back to the drawing board.

240
00:16:27,680 --> 00:16:28,960
We said we need to find something different.

241
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:36,480
And we kind of concluded that our best shot really is to create such a sticky user experience that users would love the experience.

242
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:46,240
And as a result, the brands, the partners that we were hoping to partner with, they'll be able to provide a lot of value in kind to make it worth their while.

243
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:57,680
So I mean the way we designed the user experience, we didn't want to create an Amazon, where you can exchange your sweatcoins for like thousands of different products.

244
00:16:58,080 --> 00:17:04,000
Not because we didn't want to, because we knew we couldn't get all those products in who would give them to us.

245
00:17:04,640 --> 00:17:10,960
So we created a small curated list where there would be like four products only every day and there would be a new product every day.

246
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:13,200
So essentially twelve products weekly.

247
00:17:13,600 --> 00:17:19,920
And we had a good enough net network to find twelve different brands that would give us some value in kind because they love the idea.

248
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:24,160
And this is how we resolved the cold start problem and we started getting those users.

249
00:17:24,640 --> 00:17:27,280
And the number of users gradually started to increase.

250
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:36,720
And then at some point in time, we just realized that some of those vendors, as we used to call them, they actually were queuing outside the door saying, Oh guys, when can you feature us?

