00:22:53,040 --> 00:22:56,480
So it's really interesting to me that the next layer that you layered on there.

314
00:22:56,640 --> 00:23:04,000
So you talked about this referral program where you give five sweatcoins for someone who invites a friend, but now you have a whole influencer hub.

315
00:23:04,640 --> 00:23:07,120
How does that look like and how did you layer that on?

316
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:10,640
Look, I mean, one thing leads to another as it usually happens.

317
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:18,240
And we kind of early, early stage, we just noticed that there were some users that would bring in like massive numbers.

318
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:21,200
And I'm not talking about like ten, twenty, thirty invitations.

319
00:23:21,680 --> 00:23:23,680
We're talking like hundreds, sometimes thousands.

320
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:25,120
So obviously we started digging.

321
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:30,640
And we realized that some of our early users would have a considerable following on social media.

322
00:23:31,040 --> 00:23:32,720
They were early adopters, they loved it.

323
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:46,480
And then they just, you know, because we provided that functionality, we kind of they were able to share the link on their profiles and then lots of users would come in because everybody loved the idea, it was so fresh and everybody kind of loved it.

324
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:47,760
And people loved to share.

325
00:23:48,160 --> 00:23:53,440
And when we realized that there were like people like this, we also realized one really interesting thing.

326
00:23:53,680 --> 00:24:00,160
At that stage, it was just the beginning of the influencer movement and there were like big influencers and there were everybody else.

327
00:24:00,480 --> 00:24:07,280
And there were some people that maybe had like 10,000 followers, which is not insignificant, but there was no way for them to monetize.

328
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:15,440
I'm not sure how familiar you are with the influencer business, but if you're an influencer, there's two major things that are really, that are really annoying.

329
00:24:15,680 --> 00:24:28,960
Firstly, when you work with a brand, usually do that through an agency, unless you're a massive influencer, and that's a hassle because the angel agency works as a kind of, you know, a bridge and they tell you what to do, what not to do.

330
00:24:29,200 --> 00:24:31,520
So it takes lots of iterations and it's pretty annoying.

331
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:48,640
And then the second thing, even if your contents got, you know, okay by the brand, then, you know, it's very, very very hard to see what kind of efficiency, whether you drove any results in stores, and if you did, how much, and when are you going to get the money?

332
00:24:49,040 --> 00:24:51,280
So the money, this, the cycle is very, very long.

333
00:24:51,680 --> 00:24:59,680
So what we realized is that there was a gap that wasn't essentially covered by anyone of that mid cap, so to speak, influence.

334
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:08,560
And those guys, and when we discovered that, we created a special marketplace or a special level that would only open up to a user.

335
00:25:08,880 --> 00:25:10,800
They invited thirty people successful.

336
00:25:11,120 --> 00:25:15,920
So you just install the app, you start working, and then once that number, which we counted in turn.

337
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:20,000
All of a sudden, you kind of reach more than thirty invitations.

338
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:27,440
Then a secret section of the marketplace opens up and you can convert your installs into a bigger, better range of prices.

339
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:35,280
And we would have, you know, 1000 invitations would buy you a PlayStation and 5000 invitations would buy you a flat screen TV or something.

340
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:37,200
I'm making up those numbers.

341
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:38,800
But that was the idea.

342
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:50,320
And when we did that, that basically started spreading like wildfire because lots of people with anywhere between 5000 and 5000 followers, which is again, not massive, but it's not insignificant either.

343
00:25:50,880 --> 00:25:56,800
They kind of had a way of essentially turning their following into something very tangible very quickly.

344
00:25:57,120 --> 00:26:19,120
And that's something that essentially prompted the idea of an influencer hub, which is in effect this is like a separate version of the app that's available for people who are very rich on invoice, which is a very similar app, but there's extended functionality, there's extended range of prizes, and there's extended service, so to speak.

345
00:26:19,360 --> 00:26:21,840
Like think of it as a VIP version of the app.

346
00:26:22,240 --> 00:26:23,360
And so fascinating.

347
00:26:23,680 --> 00:26:34,560
Have you since then also layered on any kind of native ad content or viral content on top of that?, that you directly work with influencers or has it all been through that influencer hub?

348
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:42,480
So look, I mean, we saw obviously we started with the influencer hub and you know, we got ourselves in a position where we didn't do any paid acquisition at all.

349
00:26:43,040 --> 00:26:44,480
Which is just so incredible.

350
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:52,800
I mean, people are going to be listening to this and well, I mean, I'll challenge giving away a slot screen TV as a form of paid acquisition, right?

351
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:54,240
With more steps.

352
00:26:54,560 --> 00:26:55,760
I agree with you, yes.

353
00:26:56,160 --> 00:27:00,640
But then again, I think that that was like that was probably counted for like five percent of all our users.

354
00:27:01,040 --> 00:27:02,320
And it fits into the model, right?

355
00:27:02,480 --> 00:27:04,320
It's part of the core loop anyway.

356
00:27:04,640 --> 00:27:05,280
It's really interesting.

357
00:27:05,440 --> 00:27:11,120
It's really interesting because then at the that stage, and that's right now, it's been written about very widely.

358
00:27:11,280 --> 00:27:13,200
I think there's like a lot of people talk about it.

359
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:15,440
Because there's a lot of viral products out there.

360
00:27:15,680 --> 00:27:24,560
The issue with viral products is that you can create an explosion, but when it dies down, like you're not in control of things because virality is uncontrollable.

361
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:26,000
It's like nuclear synthesis.

362
00:27:26,080 --> 00:27:27,760
It's basically a nuclear reaction.

363
00:27:28,400 --> 00:27:34,720
You can have an explosion, but how do you create a nuclear power station where you can control the nuclear reaction?

364
00:27:35,040 --> 00:27:43,520
And that was the biggest challenge for us, because we could have that massive spike, like thirty thousand users daily, and then it would just go back to like two or three.

365
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:54,080
I remember then I was talking to one kind of very, very smart data scientist and I said, can you build us a model that would help us understand how we create our virality?

366
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:57,760
And he was a specialist in stochastic processes, whatever that means.

367
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:08,400
And he said, look, he did it, he did some studying and then he said, well, one thing I can do, the only thing I can do, I can tell you that the virality or the viral spike is inevitable.

368
00:28:09,040 --> 00:28:10,960
One thing I cannot do, I cannot tell you when.

369
00:28:11,520 --> 00:28:15,920
And that wasn't particularly kind of useful because, fair enough, you just sit there, just waiting.

370
00:28:16,160 --> 00:28:19,360
Maybe it's going to happen tomorrow, maybe it's going to happen in three months, but you don't know.

371
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:22,720
So anyway, we kind of started doing building that sort of approach.

372
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:32,640
Now, what we did then is we just realized that there are certain stages in creating that controlled nuclear reaction.

373
00:28:33,040 --> 00:28:36,480
And the first stage is you need to kickstart the process.

374
00:28:36,960 --> 00:28:48,800
You need to create what we call a ground swell, the ground swell where you create a little bit of that noise around the brand so that people who have some following, they start listening.

375
00:28:49,200 --> 00:28:53,520
They kind of they know about the product, they've heard about it because that's important.

376
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:59,360
And that is best done when you do it through the influencers that you're friendly with.

377
00:28:59,840 --> 00:29:04,720
But those influencers need to develop a content that's very native to the channel they use.

378
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:08,400
Because at the very early days of Instagram, it was very different.

379
00:29:08,800 --> 00:29:14,560
You know, lots of people would post content that would be not necessarily kind of seen as genuine.

380
00:29:15,200 --> 00:29:18,160
Lots of professionally developed content early days again.

381
00:29:18,640 --> 00:29:21,120
We now know that, for example, TikTok is like famous.

382
00:29:21,440 --> 00:29:23,280
Like you cannot fool users there.

383
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:23,840
They will...

384
00:29:24,480 --> 00:29:26,640
you know, they will spot a fake mile out.

385
00:29:27,120 --> 00:29:28,880
So we started experimenting.

386
00:29:29,040 --> 00:29:31,360
We started working with those influencers that we were friendly with.

387
00:29:32,160 --> 00:29:38,320
And then when you kind of you start developing that content, you become really tuned to what the users need in those channels.

388
00:29:38,640 --> 00:29:54,160
And then slowly but surely, we've developed that expertise where we kind of we were able to work with influencers, then take the content, there was a winning content they would produce, and we A would share it with other influencers, you know, help them develop their own, just give them some ideas.

389
00:29:54,480 --> 00:29:57,440
And some of them we started using in our paid advertising.

390
00:29:57,680 --> 00:30:05,760
Like our paid ads constitute right now around about 10% of the total still only, but that's a very important kind of starting point.

391
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:07,440
This is how we create the ground swirl.

392
00:30:07,920 --> 00:30:20,960
From the ground swirl, we then accelerated and what's really important., probably the key, the truly unique part of the growth engine, is that when you create the virality, then the velocity of invites drops sharply.

393
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:27,840
What that means, what's velocity, is the time that is between one user installing, inviting another one, the next user installing.

394
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:38,720
Because guess what, when the velocity of invites shrinks, you could have six or seven people, or sometimes more, in the chain happening within 24 hours.

395
00:30:39,120 --> 00:30:48,000
And when that happens, the algorithms start to pick you up and you get lots of organic, kind of organic through app stores and Google and everything.

396
00:30:48,640 --> 00:31:02,800
So essentially just creating that, you know, this sequence of steps where you start creating your grounds while, where you get the influencers working with you, they develop native content, then it's being picked up because again, our user to user virality is very strong.

397
00:31:03,280 --> 00:31:11,520
The moment you create that spike, then it's being picked up and maintained by the user to user virality, and then the organics kick in.

398
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:22,240
And you become the number one health and fitness app and sometimes occasionally the global, you know, we were the number one app globally in the US, Brazil, 65 other countries have not mistaken.

399
00:31:22,560 --> 00:31:28,240
So like, you know, of course you can't sustain it, but you can sustain it the number one place in the category, for example.

400
00:31:28,640 --> 00:31:37,440
I mean, you can't sustain any one spike, but like this is sort of goes back to just app store marketing in general, which is like, you know, getting featured or whatever.

401
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:45,600
If you can create a somewhat repeatable process of knowing, oh, we'll generate so many viral spikes a year, that starts to become like a somewhat of a reliable channel.

402
00:31:45,920 --> 00:31:46,480
That's not easy.

403
00:31:46,640 --> 00:31:50,000
And we kind of we've fine tuned this growth engine over the years.

404
00:31:51,040 --> 00:31:55,840
And right now we are like literally we don't have that many spikes almost at all.

405
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:56,960
Like we're very stable.

406
00:31:57,120 --> 00:31:58,000
Yeah, but it takes time.

407
00:31:58,160 --> 00:31:58,960
It takes experimentation.

408
00:31:59,360 --> 00:32:00,000
We haven't seen it.

409
00:32:00,320 --> 00:32:04,800
There was a period there during 2020, 2021, where we would see a lot more viral spikes in apps.

410
00:32:04,880 --> 00:32:06,400
I don't think we see that as much, David.

411
00:32:06,720 --> 00:32:12,320
I mean, our famous widget kit is our friend, a very famous example, went like mega viral.

412
00:32:12,560 --> 00:32:18,720
Was that in 2021, 2020, something like, and just like nothing I'd ever seen before in terms of like how fast it went.

413
00:32:19,040 --> 00:32:22,480
And yeah, it's been a little bit, I feel like more metered since then.

414
00:32:22,640 --> 00:32:26,720
I I don't know, maybe it's more people have rushed into influencer marketing and stuff like that.

415
00:32:26,880 --> 00:32:29,840
It's become a little more competitive, but it's an interesting observation.

416
00:32:30,240 --> 00:32:33,280
Yeah, it's so fascinating building that growth engine.

417
00:32:33,600 --> 00:32:43,920
I love the analogy too of like controlling a nuclear reaction and then figuring out how to get that spark and then how to like keep getting that spark to like maintain that growth.

418
00:32:44,400 --> 00:32:58,400
But I think that, you know, the next question then is like, okay, this is an incredible growth engine and being able to acquire so many users at such a relatively cheap cost compared to a lot of other folks in the industry is just an incredible leg up.

419
00:32:58,800 --> 00:33:01,280
But then how does Sweatcoin actually make money?

420
00:33:01,520 --> 00:33:05,920
Like, how do you then monetize those free users that you've brought in?

421
00:33:06,480 --> 00:33:17,360
So look, I mean, as I said, the two main monetization channels are the commercial partnerships with brands and then the kind of the ads that I described, which we've kind of fine tuned over the years.

422
00:33:17,920 --> 00:33:22,640
However, it was very, very clear that the advertising model will only take us that far.

423
00:33:22,960 --> 00:33:27,440
Even the much bigger user base type of products, they kind of struggle with it a little bit.

424
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:28,640
Like, you know, look at Twitter.

425
00:33:29,200 --> 00:33:30,560
You know, they decided to pivot.

426
00:33:31,200 --> 00:33:40,560
And I think that, you know, for us, it was essential to be independent from the healthcare partnerships, the insurance partnerships, the corporate wellness.

427
00:33:40,880 --> 00:33:42,320
We know we'll go there one day.

428
00:33:43,040 --> 00:33:47,040
But I think there's a far kind of the much lower hanging fruit, and that is subscription.

429
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:52,160
Because, you know, with subscription, I think the name of the game is how cheaply you can acquire users at scale.

430
00:33:52,560 --> 00:33:56,880
Because everyone at one point or another, they face the music.

431
00:33:57,040 --> 00:34:01,280
They kind of, they end up in a situation where you kind of, you know, you continue to grow your CAC.

432
00:34:01,360 --> 00:34:02,160
And it's very, very hard.

433
00:34:03,600 --> 00:34:15,280
It probably took us a bit longer because I think that we probably got a little bit too excited about the fact that we got to profitability at the time when everyone else was just essentially crashing and burning.

434
00:34:15,440 --> 00:34:17,840
Like, you know, we got to profitability at the very beginning of COVID.

435
00:34:18,560 --> 00:34:22,880
And I think that I probably just, you know, got a little bit over excited as I said.

436
00:34:23,120 --> 00:34:29,680
So I think that we probably took that decision to go a subscription maybe a year, eighteen months too late in retrospect.

437
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:35,440
But I think right now what's totally amazing is that we launched a very basic subscription.

438
00:34:35,680 --> 00:34:43,360
We did a very kind of very, very kind of low grade type of development and we already have considerable amount ready.

439
00:34:43,600 --> 00:34:47,520
So from that perspective, that's the really important and exciting opportunity there.

440
00:34:47,840 --> 00:34:54,640
And then beyond that subscription, so of course you've got that like, you know, we worked with the NHS, which is the National Health Service in the UK.

441
00:34:55,040 --> 00:35:00,560
You know, these guys essentially the number one spend line for them is diabetes type 2.

442
00:35:01,120 --> 00:35:04,480
Eleven percent of the total budget of the NHS goes into diabetes type 2.

443
00:35:04,800 --> 00:35:14,320
And the NHS developed what they call the National Diabetes Prevention Program, NDPP, and that's administered to users through the kind of private providers.

444
00:35:15,360 --> 00:35:17,600
And that NDPP actually works.

445
00:35:17,760 --> 00:35:25,360
It's administered to people with prediabetic state, and that aims to essentially drive them away from the edge of the cliff.

446
00:35:25,680 --> 00:35:30,080
The problem with the existing program is that its completion rate is very low.

447
00:35:30,160 --> 00:35:38,720
It's about twenty to twenty five percent, which means that the other 75 to 80 percent of patients they end up with diabetes 2 with probability close to 1.

448
00:35:39,200 --> 00:35:47,840
Now, when Sweat Coin participates and plays a role of an engagement platform for the NHS., that completion rate goes from 25% to 89%.

449
00:35:48,240 --> 00:35:54,320
This is how we work, you know, with a partner that essentially provides the extra liquidity, so to speak.

450
00:35:54,560 --> 00:36:05,760
They provide an extra range of products that makes the whole experience for the user even more exciting because, in average, a Sweetcoin user becomes 20% more active using a free version of the app.

451
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:19,680
But let's say in the case of the NHS, when they come in and fund an additional range of prizes and other incentives, that number goes up to 45% and users lose about 6% of their body weight in 10 weeks and all this kind of just.

452
00:36:20,080 --> 00:36:23,680
So the model is there., but the B2B is a very different beast.

453
00:36:23,920 --> 00:36:26,640
Is the NHS doing anything to promote actual installation?

454
00:36:27,280 --> 00:36:31,520
I don't want to go into discussing how good the NHS are in terms of digital.

455
00:36:32,160 --> 00:36:32,880
Well, I don't know.

456
00:36:32,960 --> 00:36:36,000
Are they distributing QR codes at doctor's offices and stuff for you guys?

457
00:36:36,960 --> 00:36:38,960
But it's purely their funding, their funding.

458
00:36:39,280 --> 00:36:41,200
No, it's it's a little bit more complex than this.

459
00:36:41,360 --> 00:36:43,680
It's just, you know, requires a deeper integration.

460
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:47,760
But why I'm saying this is that there's unlimited opportunities in the B2B space.

461
00:36:47,920 --> 00:36:52,480
But I think that just generally speaking, we still want to continue to be true to our rules of the B2C.

462
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:56,640
And from that perspective, you know, this paid subscription is such an obvious.

463
00:36:56,960 --> 00:37:02,640
And, you know, look, you know, at this this stage we are running about, you know, two thirds of all our user base is actually free.

464
00:37:03,040 --> 00:37:06,720
And we onboard anywhere between like 60,000 and 80,000 users every day.

465
00:37:06,800 --> 00:37:07,680
It's a massive scale.

466
00:37:08,080 --> 00:37:11,360
As is for any health and fitness product, retention is the name of the game.

467
00:37:11,600 --> 00:37:15,600
You kind of move retention by one percent and that's a massive uplift.

468
00:37:15,920 --> 00:37:21,120
But just coming back to that paid subscription conversation, look, you know, there's a, there's a lot of opportunity in there.

469
00:37:21,440 --> 00:37:22,320
And we've just started.

470
00:37:22,400 --> 00:37:23,440
We're scratching the surface.

471
00:37:23,600 --> 00:37:24,400
There's plenty of stuff.

472
00:37:24,720 --> 00:37:37,280
I think that the good thing there is that a lot of people, as you already kind of mentioned, you know, a lot of people in the subscription base space, they start with subs and then they hit a ceiling and then they try to diversify into other ready ready streams.

473
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:43,680
I think with us, you know, subscriptions in a funny way, it's a much more well understood path.

474
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:46,000
And a lot of people have done amazing things.

475
00:37:46,320 --> 00:37:52,080
So it's relatively easier for us just, you know, to take a couple of pages from their book and continue to build.

476
00:37:52,560 --> 00:37:54,640
Yeah, I was just looking at the premium offering.

477
00:37:54,800 --> 00:38:04,320
It much more matches like what you would see, like a Pokemon Go or like some other games that layer on subscriptions, just like you get premium access to stuff that people don't get.

478
00:38:04,640 --> 00:38:06,960
It's not necessarily like a content and feature unlock.

479
00:38:07,200 --> 00:38:12,240
It's like a boost to your ability to play the game, which I think fits really well, you know.

480
00:38:12,480 --> 00:38:20,160
And it becomes a no brainer for someone who's very serious about this, like to put in a little bit of money and you'll get a lot of leverage out of your usage.

481
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:22,480
You think of things like a family subscription.

482
00:38:22,560 --> 00:38:30,720
Like, you know, one of the things that very early days, Michael founded, he actually packed his the pocket money to his kids to the number of sweat quads they were generating.

483
00:38:31,040 --> 00:38:32,000
Yeah, I got to do this.

484
00:38:32,480 --> 00:38:33,600
There's so much we can do.

485
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:39,760
Because, you know, at the end of the day, walking is the type of activity that's available to almost everyone.

486
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:42,000
You know, that's almost like breathing.

487
00:38:42,560 --> 00:38:47,840
And it's such an endless area of opportunity and physical activity.

488
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:49,120
And again, because of me.

489
00:38:49,120 --> 00:38:55,040
Because of many of my friends who are in the technology sector, we are moving less and less because we're slaves to convenience.

490
00:38:55,600 --> 00:39:01,520
You know, it all started with the remote control, but right now you don't have to step outside to buy food anymore.

491
00:39:01,920 --> 00:39:03,360
