492
00:39:03,840 --> 00:39:09,920
So before you know it, we're all in Wally kind of, you know, we're all moving in those capsules.

493
00:39:10,160 --> 00:39:12,720
I got to go to a Sam's Club and ride one of the scooters the other day.

494
00:39:12,880 --> 00:39:14,080
Unrelated but related.

495
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:15,600
So I got to experience it.

496
00:39:16,080 --> 00:39:16,880
That's scary, man.

497
00:39:17,040 --> 00:39:17,280
I know.

498
00:39:17,440 --> 00:39:24,800
I was like, oh, your point earlier about there being no natural incentive to, you know, I was like, if everyone had a scooter, you just would, you know, superior.

499
00:39:25,120 --> 00:39:27,360
For those who have zero context on Twitter.

500
00:39:27,680 --> 00:39:31,280
Jacob broke his foot like a month ago and had a massive surgery.

501
00:39:31,840 --> 00:39:34,000
And so even more sedentary than usual.

502
00:39:34,160 --> 00:39:35,840
You might have to give me a dang sweat coin.

503
00:39:35,920 --> 00:39:36,240
I'll go.

504
00:39:36,400 --> 00:39:37,760
I would love to go ten miles right now.

505
00:39:37,920 --> 00:39:38,560
That would be amazing.

506
00:39:39,760 --> 00:39:41,440
But now I have a double reason.

507
00:39:41,760 --> 00:39:50,080
So Jacob kind of alluded to it, but I wanted to dig a little deeper into how you thought about laying value into the subscription.

508
00:39:50,240 --> 00:40:00,800
And as you mentioned, even you know, you kind of are doing the reverse of what so many people in the industry do of like spending a ton on paid ads, putting people directly into subscription.

509
00:40:01,120 --> 00:40:11,280
Many apps are even doing hard paywalls now, apps are switching to hard paywalls, but then you hit a ceiling and then you have to figure out, oh gosh, how do we get our customer acquisition costs down?

510
00:40:12,160 --> 00:40:13,840
How do we layer on a freemium product?

511
00:40:14,080 --> 00:40:22,960
So you've kind of done this in the opposite, but I think it would be really interesting to hear how you thought about creating value for users in that subscription.

512
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:30,080
Like what's that kind of primary motivation for someone to actually pay when the freemium product is so good?

513
00:40:30,640 --> 00:40:34,160
Look, first of all, we've built a product with inherent limitations.

514
00:40:34,640 --> 00:40:39,760
Like there's only that type of products that the free version can afford, so to speak.

515
00:40:40,240 --> 00:40:46,160
There's, you know, we would always set a ceiling on the number of sweatcoins you can earn on a given day.

516
00:40:46,640 --> 00:40:51,920
So because of that, you know, we needed to create a kind of economy that would be sustainable.

517
00:40:52,560 --> 00:40:58,880
So we introduced a bunch of restrictions early on that we actually can lift or remove.

518
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:06,560
One of those restrictions, so to speak, because, you know, we talked a lot about the ads and I was kind of very excited about it.

519
00:41:06,800 --> 00:41:15,360
But then let's face it, some of the users, even those users that are not engaged with those apps, the ads, they kind of see the ad and it's not necessarily the ideal experience.

520
00:41:15,840 --> 00:41:19,680
So I think removing ads to some users, that's a massive value proposition.

521
00:41:19,760 --> 00:41:25,520
I'm not saying that's the only thing we're going to do, but essentially just removing the restrictions that are inherent to the free experience.

522
00:41:25,920 --> 00:41:27,040
I think that's a massive one.

523
00:41:27,520 --> 00:41:35,280
But then there's a bunch of other features that are so to speak more expensive and cannot be justified for the free user.

524
00:41:35,600 --> 00:41:48,560
A case in point, like we are working on a walking coach, is someone who essentially helps you with, provides the nudges, provides those not necessarily insights, no, a robot, of course, not a human.

525
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:50,960
Like you cannot justify a human who really is a user.

526
00:41:51,360 --> 00:41:54,240
So there's a bunch of different things that can only be available to a PayStation.

527
00:41:55,120 --> 00:42:05,600
And that arises very naturally from the user experience because, you know, I we always think about Sweat Coin as a platformic experience on top of which you can build so much more.

528
00:42:06,400 --> 00:42:18,080
And like, that's like literally we've built that very platformic use case where it's very simple, you walk, your steps are being converted, Sweat Coin's being issued, and there's a bunch of basic things you can do with them.

529
00:42:18,320 --> 00:42:18,640
That's all.

530
00:42:18,960 --> 00:42:20,480
And then you can start building, okay.

531
00:42:20,880 --> 00:42:22,800
There's other ways, alternative ways to earn.

532
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:24,560
There's alternative ways to spend.

533
00:42:24,960 --> 00:42:28,560
There's alternative ways to kind of do something with that Sweat Coin.

534
00:42:29,120 --> 00:42:33,920
And that all very nicely feeds into the paid subscription idea.

535
00:42:34,480 --> 00:42:54,880
Now, the way I like to think about, you know, this, and I know it sounds a bit controversial because most of the market would say, yeah, you can start at top and then you kind of like you launch a more expensive car and then you can create a mass market car, but sometimes you can like launch a Southwest and then you can create like a premium experience.

536
00:42:55,200 --> 00:43:04,080
And the premium experience will be the same plane, just wider seats, better quality food, better service, and people would be prepared to pay.

537
00:43:04,480 --> 00:43:08,800
Because at the end of the day, like all our users, we divide them broadly into into two groups.

538
00:43:09,360 --> 00:43:12,560
Those that come for rewards and those that come for health.

539
00:43:13,120 --> 00:43:21,920
Those that come for rewards are less sticky, but some, a considerable chunk of them, they actually qualify into what we call come for rewards state for health.

540
00:43:22,160 --> 00:43:27,520
They come for their free stuff and then before you know it, they go like, oh man, I'm walking forty percent more.

541
00:43:27,680 --> 00:43:28,320
Wow, that's cool.

542
00:43:28,560 --> 00:43:29,760
I'm no longer kind of interested.

543
00:43:29,920 --> 00:43:31,520
Yeah, I'm past rewards, but you know what?

544
00:43:31,680 --> 00:43:32,880
I'm hooked up on walking.

545
00:43:33,280 --> 00:43:34,160
How do you distinguish?

546
00:43:34,320 --> 00:43:39,120
Like, what are the signals you see that tells you, like, what category someone's in based on behaviors or?

547
00:43:39,520 --> 00:43:41,360
Well, I mean, we do a lot of user research.

548
00:43:41,680 --> 00:43:48,560
We observe a lot of kind of user behavior patterns, like those users who are engage with ads more, they tend to be more rewards focused.

549
00:43:49,120 --> 00:43:56,160
There are some users, actually, it's incredible how the balance itself serves as a progression system.

550
00:43:56,880 --> 00:44:00,400
Because, you know, it's actually what we've done, and, you know, it's really amazing.

551
00:44:00,480 --> 00:44:08,720
We just reframe steps because, you know, we used to have Fitbit, and Fitbit is wonderful, but then you have kind of ten thousand steps, next morning zero to ten thousand steps.

552
00:44:08,880 --> 00:44:10,480
Afterwards, it's just shit, man, it's come on.

553
00:44:10,560 --> 00:44:12,160
It's like a, it's the same thing again.

554
00:44:12,480 --> 00:44:21,200
The moment you reframe and you say, these are not steps, these are coins, and here's your balance, and your balance keeps on growing, and you actually quite reach.

555
00:44:21,760 --> 00:44:24,480
And I know it for myself, even now, I'm looking at my balance.

556
00:44:25,200 --> 00:44:28,560
My balance is like, what have I, ten, fifteen thousand sweatcoins.

557
00:44:28,960 --> 00:44:29,520
Makes me feel good.

558
00:44:29,920 --> 00:44:30,480
And bought in.

559
00:44:30,800 --> 00:44:32,960
Like there's like literal lock in there.

560
00:44:33,280 --> 00:44:35,760
Yeah, man, I've got a lot of sweatcoins.

561
00:44:35,840 --> 00:44:37,040
I really want to get to twenty thousand.

562
00:44:37,440 --> 00:44:37,760
I'm rich.

563
00:44:38,240 --> 00:44:43,040
So I think that that reframing, that's a major, that's a massive, that's a very powerful thing.

564
00:44:43,520 --> 00:44:51,040
And I think that that's, you know, coming back to what we started with, you know, understanding behavioral economics, behavioral finance generally, I think it's useful.

565
00:44:51,120 --> 00:44:51,200
Yeah.

566
00:44:51,360 --> 00:44:53,520
I mean, I think there's lessons there for anyone building an app.

567
00:44:54,080 --> 00:44:57,120
It doesn't necessarily have to be direct to a coin or some analogy like that.

568
00:44:57,360 --> 00:45:05,200
But the more that a user is's experience can vary with relation to the amount of time and effort they've invested in your app.

569
00:45:05,520 --> 00:45:06,880
Like you'll create those hooks, right?

570
00:45:07,040 --> 00:45:09,040
And that has downstream effects on retention.

571
00:45:09,200 --> 00:45:11,600
And it doesn't even have to be all like there's no logic there.

572
00:45:11,760 --> 00:45:14,400
There's really no, there's no literal value.

573
00:45:14,720 --> 00:45:16,720
But like I guess literal value is not a thing, right?

574
00:45:16,880 --> 00:45:18,000
It's all in the eye of the beholder.

575
00:45:18,160 --> 00:45:21,440
So if that, if you value having a high balance, it now has value, right?

576
00:45:21,840 --> 00:45:22,080
Correct.

577
00:45:22,400 --> 00:45:26,400
Most of that because a lot of people get a little bit too literal talking about the value of SWOTCOIN.

578
00:45:26,480 --> 00:45:31,440
And we always like to think and differentiate the kind of the rational value element and then the emotional value.

579
00:45:31,760 --> 00:45:39,040
What your sweatcoins at?

580
00:45:39,920 --> 00:45:41,600
Like, what's the exchange rate?

581
00:45:42,000 --> 00:45:45,040
How much would you buy for someone else's sweatcoin?

582
